d with him there said he was a  person of such enormous faith he would  always find a positive in something even  when there wasn't very much positive and  CBS News president susan Zorinsky said  John Lane was a teacher of our craft on  the road he might get a little loud when  giving you feedback but he was so ready  and willing to help you along the path  he made journalism more than a job he  made it a calling John Lane was 89 Gail  yeah John Lane makes it a very personal  story for us once again here at CBS but  the thing I so liked about your piece  Anthony that you do every Friday it's  just such a reminder of how this disease  operates I mean when you look at the  ages of the people that we've lost and  you look at the ethnicity of the people  that we've lost and the the sexes of the  people of the gender of the people that  we lost it really does not discriminate  and it's such a reminder of how we  really do have to follow the rules those  pieces touch me every single week I  think it's so important that we do to  remind that there are people behind  these numbers that we report on every  day yeah yeah there's enormous human  cost every life lost is worth  remembering we continue to tell these  stories on our broadcast and social  media platforms if you want us to share  the story of someone close to you please  tell us you can email coronavirus at CBS  news.com
 

